00:00:26.075 --> 00:00:27.200
AMY BANZAERT: Hi, everyone.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.740
Today we're going to be
doing trip prep and review.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.630
And so the first
part of trip prep--

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.180
this would work better if
I were actually projecting,

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.980
wouldn't it?

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.210
[CHUCKLES] So trip
prep and review.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.560
And the first thing we're
going to do is presentation.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.950
So each team has 15
minutes to present,

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.920
and then 5 to 10
minutes of questions.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.060
And then we'll go through a
review of a few things that

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.820
got missed or badly
presented previously,

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.760
and then going over trip
prep, and then there

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.260
will be a little
bit of time for you

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.750
guys to work in teams and
a few critical things.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.720
I also need to talk to every
single person who's traveling,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:08.710
just one on one.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.070
It's a D-Lab requirement.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.670
So we'll do that.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.332
Yeah, are there any
questions before we

00:01:15.332 --> 00:01:16.540
start with the presentations?

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.980
OK, cool.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.950
So AsoFenix, if you
guys want to go first,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.870
that would be awesome.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.990
GUEST SPEAKER: All
right, so we're AsoFenix.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:29.790
I'm Carter.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.790
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm Buzz.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:31.900
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm Rosser.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.890
GUEST SPEAKER: Brianna.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.830
GUEST SPEAKER: And our
team mentor is Kem.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:40.570
So AsoFenix is a
small Nicaraguan NGO.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.280
We're based in two areas--

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.600
El Roblar and Potreitos.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.102
And there are four products
that we're looking at right now.

00:01:49.102 --> 00:01:50.560
One of them is the
OJ Sealer, which

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.570
you guys might be aware of.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.900
The next would be the Millet
Thresher, the Washing Machine,

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.350
and the Biodigester.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.030
So I'm going to
hand it off to Buzz.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.930
GUEST SPEAKER: So the OJ
sealer is a project that

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.680
was a D-Lab project previously.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.930
And we're going to deliver it.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:12.500
So I should be giving you the
group names, but I'm sorry.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.020
I don't have that right now.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.280
But it was a previous--

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.630
it's not our project, so I don't
want to take credit for it.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:23.890
But we are delivering it to
the community down there.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:28.750
And the purpose is to be able to
seal orange juice so that they

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:34.330
can sell it at a later date
during the harvest when oranges

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.640
are just so plentiful
that the cost really

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.740
isn't worth sealing.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.860
So if they can bag the juice
and then sell it later,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.250
they'll be able to make
money from their harvest.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.480
So how it works is you have--

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.770
it's kind of like the
sealers we use in the lab.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.170
And you bring down
this lever here,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.280
and then you can latch it.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.100
There's a latch that
secures it in place,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.260
and that's a safety
feature, and also ensures

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.780
a good connection between
the metal and the plastic

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.460
so that you get a good seal
and also you can't just

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.410
jolt it or lift
it up accidentally

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:18.190
and then you end up with a
mess and possibly an injury.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.420
So there's a latch.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.090
There's a switch that
you press and then

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.330
it heats for 10 seconds.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.420
You can adjust that time.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.780
We'll have to, I guess,
optimize that time.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.100
It's set to 10 seconds now.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.440
From what I understand,
that's arbitrary.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.390
So we might adjust that time.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.070
And there's an
LED that lights up

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:43.810
while the rod is heating so that
the user knows that it is hot

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.600
and not to undo the
latch and open the rod.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.835
So this project-- and
10 seconds of heating,

00:03:51.835 --> 00:03:55.450
and the metal that's used
heats up very quickly

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.710
and also cools off very quickly.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.700
So after that 10
seconds is over,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.590
you allow, like, 5
seconds of cooling,

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:10.040
and then you can open
the lever safely.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.110
So we're going to go down and
implement the project and some

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.930
of the instructions
that we'll have to give

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.290
and how to handle the
plastic waste that they have,

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.280
because they're most likely
not accustomed to having

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.820
the sheets of plastic and what
to make sure they understand

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.570
the consequences of
littering in the environment,

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.660
and then also explain
the operations.

00:04:38.385 --> 00:04:38.885
Oh,

00:04:38.885 --> 00:04:40.160
GUEST SPEAKER: You're next.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.660
GUEST SPEAKER: So
when we were first

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.850
getting the groups in
the projects and stuff,

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.470
there was mention
of a feed chopper.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.845
Well after talking
with AsoFenix,

00:04:46.845 --> 00:04:49.410
we can tell they're
actually interested in is

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.320
it a different part
of harvest processing

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.600
is threshing their millet crop.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.260
And that means is taking all
the little seeds are within,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.310
they call them florets--

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.460
little stalks on the big one--
and removing all the seeds from

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:09.730
it and separating the stuff you
can eat from stuff you can't.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.850
So these are a couple
different designs

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.600
that have been built
by various groups.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:22.510
They're both-- you can't see
the actual mechanism that's

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.200
doing the threshing
in either of them,

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.120
but it's basically they've
got little spikes on.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.210
I think this one has two
drums inside each other

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.000
with little spikes that are
just knocking the seeds out.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.690
And this one they've taken
the tire off the wheel,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.260
and they're using all
the spokes sticking out

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.610
of the frame of the
wheel to pick it off.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:51.650
Since we only found out what
the project was very recently,

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.600
we haven't done much thinking
about our own designs

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.400
with this stuff.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.020
We're wondering if these
are kind of capacity though.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:08.260
So mainly on the trip we're
going to be getting information

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:09.978
about the process,
since it's not

00:06:09.978 --> 00:06:11.770
something we're familiar
with what you need

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.370
to do to thresh the millet.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.420
Their harvest is 4,000 pounds.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:22.240
So we need to know how much
that actually is so you

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.700
an idea of how much we need
to be threshing at the time.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.580
And then there are
different varieties.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.080
We need to know
what variety they're

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.350
using, which will
help determine what

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.360
the appropriate method
of threshing it is.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.720
How long they need to do it--
and they're currently doing it

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.920
by hand--

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.000
and how efficient is this?

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.140
There was discussion
about moving the machine

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:45.530
between different communities.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.460
And so whether the
different communities

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.140
are harvesting at
the same time or not

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.337
is going to affect how
fast it needs to work,

00:06:53.337 --> 00:06:55.420
because if everyone's
harvesting at the same time,

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.620
that's more people that need
to get their harvest done

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.550
within a smaller amount of time.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.230
But if somebody a few miles
away is lower elevation

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.690
and has the harvest
slightly earlier,

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.040
that gives us more time spread.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.540
So the preliminary specs--

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.240
they're currently taking four
days to thresh the harvest.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.230
We could get that
down to eight hours.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:24.130
All of these, I'm not going to
go through and read them all

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.670
because they're mostly very
arbitrary at this point.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:31.510
And for this project,
it's going to be

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:33.363
a lot of information gathering.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.050
GUEST SPEAKER: OK, so the next
project we wanted to look at

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.790
was a washing machine.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.250
Interestingly enough, we were
kind of so gung-ho for this

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.700
that we kind of leapt through
and did the most planning.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.890
And it turns out that
AsoFenix said, no, we'd

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.360
rather have focus on this.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.800
So, you know, that's part
of the planning process.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:53.840
But in any case--

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:54.618
oh, shoot.

00:07:54.618 --> 00:07:55.910
Did the chart not come through?

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.170
Anyway.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.320
So some key things
we want to keep

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.980
in mind for the washing machine
are the preservation of water.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.340
There might not be that much
water to use in this community.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.920
AsoFenix really also wants
to utilize the gray water

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.617
to do something useful,
rather than just dumping it.

00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:14.450
We also have a couple
of different variables

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.140
to consider.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.970
So D-Lab has done a lot of
work with washing machines

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:20.480
in the past.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.570
This is one of the designs
they've come up with.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:26.690
Benji is not here right now,
but he also showed us one

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.970
that he did for
D-Lab design that

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:32.429
was a similar design like this.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:36.320
So we kind of took
this and ran with it.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.770
Yes, so this is
basically the same idea,

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.230
except using direct
drive from a bicycle that

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:44.630
can be detached and then
still used as a bicycle,

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.700
instead of a bicycle
that's gutted for parts

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.950
and mounted directly
to the thing.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.170
The key variable we'd
like to test here

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.368
is whether to do
side- or top-loading.

00:08:53.368 --> 00:08:55.160
That kind of depends
how efficient and easy

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.480
it is to load.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.520
We'd also like to alter and
play with the inner drum design.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.770
Should we make it out of rope?

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.360
Should we make it
out of chicken wire?

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.140
We were thinking for a while
of doing nylon bags that

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.190
would be put in place.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.760
So that would spin and
be their own rotors.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.040
And then also the draining
methods, though again,

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.550
we can preserve as much
gray water as we can.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:20.030
Sorry, I had a spec sheet,
and apparently my open Office

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.470
format kind of ate it.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.302
But they're all intuitive.

00:09:24.302 --> 00:09:25.760
GUEST SPEAKER: And
then the product

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.355
that AsoFenix wanted us to
focus on was the biodigester.

00:09:30.355 --> 00:09:31.730
So what this is
is basically they

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.240
wanted us to find a way
to efficiently mix up

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:40.010
the slew based off their
current concrete and plastic bag

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:40.960
method.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:41.960
So here are spec sheets.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.910
Basically, you want it
to be hand-powered or

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.080
bicycle-powered.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.110
The main requirement being
there's no electricity needed.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.270
We need something that
stays in the same scale

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:55.130
that they already have,
4 to 10 cubic meters.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:56.840
And then after
one use, they want

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.020
to produce about 1
cubic meter of biogas

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:03.810
and take about two or
three hours to complete.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.240
Those are the main specs that
they wanted us to achieve.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.240
Upon further discussions
with AsoFenix,

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.390
they showed us that this
is their current design,

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:16.100
and they wanted us
to actually not focus

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:20.990
on a method of making the
slew, but actually finding

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:24.140
a way or a method to
determine overflow,

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.560
improve their current
process, and find a way

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:30.410
to gauge how much biogas their
producing at any given time.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:31.910
So right now what's
happening is you

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.702
can see the plastic
bags in the background,

00:10:33.702 --> 00:10:36.290
and the biogas is delivered
through this tube from right

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:37.980
to left.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.530
And as there's an overflow, it
goes down this pipe and bubbles

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:43.167
in the water.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:45.500
So if any user were to see
bubbles throughout the water,

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:50.090
they would know that there's
being overflow usage.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:53.390
That being said, you don't
know how much is in the tubes

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:55.370
until it's too much.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.930
This can only determine whether
it's overflowing or not,

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:02.270
and you can only tell
that if you're there

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:03.360
and see the bubbles.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.660
So if you're asleep
and it starts bubbling,

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.350
you won't know
this is happening.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.080
And so they want to
find a new method

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.450
to determine how much
biogas is being produced.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:16.190
So we changed the whole project
in regards to this, instead

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:20.480
of a mixer, and started looking
at the manometer pressure gauge

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.070
system of how to determine
how much gas is there.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:27.610
So we're thinking about throwing
basically just a rubber tube,

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.730
having a fixed diameter
circular objects,

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:33.820
such as a soda can, plastic
bottle, so we have a fixed

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.500
diameter that's common there.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.860
And we'd wrap the
plastic tubing around it

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.760
and fill it up with water.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.120
And we'd measure, make sure
the water levels are even.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.670
And then we would
release the valve,

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:48.610
so we can see the pressure
difference between the water.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:53.860
And that head difference is how
much pressure there is inside.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:58.150
And then we'll have markings
saying, if it's this much head

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:00.730
difference, then there
will be blank amount

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:06.298
of pressure, which means
0.6 cubic meters of biogas.

00:12:06.298 --> 00:12:07.840
And then every day
they can check out

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.810
how much biogas there is because
of the pressure difference.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:15.370
And then once they hit a
certain head loss difference,

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.370
then they will know they're
reaching their overflow

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.240
capacity.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.200
This will help them
determine how much they have

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.907
and how much they can use.

00:12:24.907 --> 00:12:27.490
So let's say if they wanted to
use some and they weren't full,

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:30.550
they wouldn't want to try to use
0.25 cubic meters if they only

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.540
have 0.1.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.130
So this is the biodigester.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.290
And some key activities--

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:40.770
basically, just a quick
summary of our projects.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.480
The OJ sealer--

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.150
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah,
so this outlines

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.820
what each project is
going to accomplish

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:48.820
while we're down there.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.810
So we're going to
deliver the OJ sealer,

