00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:14.900
have some kind of parabolic
mirror, which reflects sunlight

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.412
into the cooker.

00:24:16.412 --> 00:24:18.620
And they're curved concentrators
because the parabola

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.030
is the curve.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.740
The second type is
a heat box, which

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:24.240
is basically just a box which
uses the greenhouse effect.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:28.160
So you have the sunlight
hitting the box and then the box

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:30.260
retaining a lot of heat.

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:33.440
And then the third type is
your panel solar cooker,

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:37.480
which is basically like a box
with a hinged mirror on it,

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:39.230
where you have sunlight
hitting the mirror

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:43.010
and then getting
reflected on to the box.

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:46.220
So those are the three types
that predominantly exist.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:50.100
When we looked into what they
have currently with them there,

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:55.730
they basically use a
hybrid between the heat box

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:59.570
heater and the panel
heater, where some designs

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:01.970
do have our panels
but then they also

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:05.000
use reflective surfaces
inside to keep it warm.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:11.280
GUEST SPEAKER: So a group
from Columbia University

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.160
has been working with the
Solar Women of Totogalpa

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.320
to develop what they
currently have now.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:21.060
And it's, as Sid said,
this box solar cooker.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.560
The one thing we
really, really want

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:25.500
to focus on when we get there
is figuring out why it's

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:28.010
so expensive to manufacture.

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:30.320
The Women really
pride themselves

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:34.880
in being able to make
this cooker on their own.

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:38.270
So we want to see how we can
work with them, what they like

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:42.320
about the cooker, and
specifically what materials

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:45.500
make it so expensive to make.

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:48.800
So we came up with
a new final design

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.710
that we might want
to improve upon.

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:57.015
And it's kind of the same, but
I think to really move forward

00:25:57.015 --> 00:25:59.390
with this we really need to
figure out the materials that

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:00.530
are available to us.

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.940
And stuff like that.

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:05.717
So want to talk about the specs?

00:26:05.717 --> 00:26:06.550
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:08.440
I just wanted to really
gear over real quick.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:10.450
So the designs,
basically we wanted

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:12.520
to do two different
types of designs.

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:14.980
One was where we were
trying to improve

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:16.630
what they have
currently, and one

00:26:16.630 --> 00:26:20.030
was we wanted to
design something new.

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:23.600
And so basically we
came up with two things.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:27.440
So this is an improvement, and
we might pass it around later.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:28.752
But this is an improvement--

00:26:28.752 --> 00:26:30.460
I think what we think
is an improvement--

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:31.550
on their current design.

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:33.250
We think it might
reduce material costs

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:35.920
and improve efficiency.

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:38.830
This design is kind
of like a mixture

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:42.430
between the curved
concentrator and the heat box.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:46.090
Essentially, this is trying
to make a pressure cooker.

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:47.960
We weren't sure if
there's a need for that.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:49.120
So that's actually
one of our questions.

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:50.912
We're wondering how
much food they actually

00:26:50.912 --> 00:26:52.930
cook using pressure cookers.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:56.070
But we didn't see any literature
about them using anything

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:57.820
like that currently,
and we were wondering

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.860
if it was possibly of any use.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.990
So those are the basic
two designs we worked on.

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:05.012
And then, Vivan, you can
talk about the project specs.

00:27:05.012 --> 00:27:05.845
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:27:05.845 --> 00:27:07.537
So some of our
specs are it needs

00:27:07.537 --> 00:27:08.620
to definitely retain heat.

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:12.450
So we'll consider different
types of insulation.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:15.110
It has to be able to reach
high temperatures whether they

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:16.860
need to be cooking
different kinds of meat

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:18.792
or just boiling water.

00:27:18.792 --> 00:27:21.000
It also has to be able to
withstand different weather

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:21.490
conditions.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.032
If it's going to be
sitting outside--

00:27:23.032 --> 00:27:25.560
because it is a solar cooker--

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.930
the climate in Nicaragua
is kind of humid,

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:29.010
and it does rain a lot.

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:30.693
So that's really important.

00:27:30.693 --> 00:27:33.360
It needs to be affordable, which
we've already mentioned before,

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:37.470
and easy to manufacture, or
fairly easy to manufacture.

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:40.698
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, we actually
came up with a bunch of them.

00:27:40.698 --> 00:27:42.990
But these were what we thought
were the most important.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:44.640
Another thing that
could play a role

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.160
is how aesthetically
pleasing it is,

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:48.960
because we did come
across literature

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:51.870
where they refused to use
certain cookers because they

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:53.658
didn't like the way
that they looked.

00:27:53.658 --> 00:27:55.200
So that's a very
important part also,

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.210
which we're trying to work on.

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:01.445
Yeah, that's pretty much what
we're working on right now.

00:28:01.445 --> 00:28:02.320
GUEST SPEAKER: Right.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:05.830
And so last but not least is
the solar cell phone charger.

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:09.790
And I'll be speaking on behalf
of me and Kurt for this one.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:13.630
So two key findings that we
had in our literature research

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:17.080
were just a comment
that solar is

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:19.690
the most flexible and
efficient of off-grid chargers.

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:21.340
And we were like,
yay, thumbs up.

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:24.190
And they gave the rule of
thumb that about 60 minutes

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:26.020
of charging would
mean 10 minutes

00:28:26.020 --> 00:28:27.190
of talking on the phone.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:29.775
Now, the specifications of what
kind of panels you're using

00:28:29.775 --> 00:28:31.900
and whatever you're not,
all that can be optimized.

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:35.540
So that led us to one of
our design considerations,

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:39.490
which is in the schematic--
basically, a communal charger,

00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:42.310
so just having instead
of one small solar panel,

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:44.650
enough to charge one
cell phone at a time,

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:47.268
having a big communal
charging station.

00:28:47.268 --> 00:28:49.060
But of course, that
requires the field work

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:50.930
of being there for
this week and realizing

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:52.930
whether these people can
actually work together,

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.480
whether there's going
to be a risk of having

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:58.598
your charger stolen or having
your cell phone battery stolen

00:28:58.598 --> 00:29:00.640
while it's being charged,
or whether you can have

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:04.840
some kind of a system where you
leave something that's yours

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:07.677
and that doesn't get stolen
while you take the battery.

00:29:07.677 --> 00:29:09.760
So seeing how that works,
what the different kinds

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:13.820
of batteries that they're
using in their cell phones are.

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:16.240
And so one idea that
also stemmed from that

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:21.760
is having a port that's
adaptable to different kinds

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.160
of cell phone chargers, because
if they change their cell

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:26.702
phone, then you're not going to
throw away the whole charging

00:29:26.702 --> 00:29:28.070
station.

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:30.070
So that's some of the
ideas that came from that.

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:31.902
And another thing we
were thinking about

00:29:31.902 --> 00:29:33.610
was the design
consideration that they're

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:36.963
going to be using their
cell phones on the fields.

00:29:36.963 --> 00:29:38.380
A lot of times
they'll leave home,

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:40.900
they'll go work on
the field, they'll

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:42.850
go look for food,
whatever it is,

00:29:42.850 --> 00:29:46.310
and they'll have their cell
phones on them for a long time.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:52.520
So if they ever have it with
them for longer than needed,

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:55.142
they won't be able to come
home and just charge it or plug

00:29:55.142 --> 00:29:57.100
in their charger that
they have in their pocket

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:58.183
into a wall outlet, right?

00:29:58.183 --> 00:29:59.030
Unrealistic.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.900
So one thing we thought is
some kind of mobile charger.

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:05.650
One technology that is a little
advanced I think for our skills

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:08.470
right now is it's a
layer of solar cells

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:11.800
that's integrated below the
LCD screens of the iPhones.

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:15.190
But we thought that
something to that effect,

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:18.020
where the women could
carry it around with them.

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:20.620
And the idea of the solar cell
phone charger for the iPhone

00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:22.300
is that you can carry
it in your pocket

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:24.603
with the LCD screen
towards the outside

00:30:24.603 --> 00:30:26.020
and the rays just
hit it, and it's

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:27.940
charging as you walk
around kind of thing.

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:29.690
So we thought that'd
be a really nice idea

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:33.280
if we could make some
kind of Velcro pouch

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.100
for the men and women
that are carrying around,

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:38.500
and then it can charge
as they go or something.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.990
So a little more
reliable of a technology,

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:43.420
but that takes a little more
research, and if they even

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:46.348
need that kind of
design consideration.

00:30:46.348 --> 00:30:47.890
So some of the
project specifications

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:51.910
that we are keeping in mind are
the ones that I just mentioned.

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:54.460
The idea of maybe
having a charger that's

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:59.200
not powered by solar
radiation, having

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.398
different types
of phones, and so

00:31:01.398 --> 00:31:03.190
instead of having
different kinds of phones

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.232
and needing to have
different kind of connections

00:31:05.232 --> 00:31:07.540
to the phone, like
what we usually use,

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:09.340
just taking out the
battery and seeing

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:12.220
how that can fit into
the adaptable ports

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:14.350
that I mentioned earlier.

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:16.603
And being able to charge
your phone day at night.

00:31:16.603 --> 00:31:19.270
We usually come home and we like
to charge our phone while we're

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:23.020
sleeping, wake up in the
morning, pick it up and go.

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:25.538
If it's a solar charger,
you would need the sunlight.

00:31:25.538 --> 00:31:26.830
So that's the little trade off.

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:31.030
But we'd be working with energy
storage in the solar panel.

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:33.340
It would be integrated so
you can charge it at night

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:35.830
and use it when you need to.

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:37.840
And finally, of
course, there's always

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:41.170
the risk, like with all of
our other projects, of theft.

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:44.500
We are going to be there next
week also assessing, like,

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:47.200
if you leave your solar panel
on the roof-- is there even

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:48.555
a roof, first of all?

00:31:48.555 --> 00:31:50.680
If you leave it on the
roof, can somebody steal it?

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:52.840
If you leave it on the roof,
is it going to be damaged?

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:55.210
Is it something you want to
be able to take in and out?

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:57.730
What if we just set up a
system where we have somebody

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:00.610
like a security guard for
this charging station?

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:03.220
And then maybe people
are willing to pitch in

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:05.500
if you have a more
efficient charging

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:08.740
mechanism with somebody guarding
the solar panels or something.

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:11.330
So lots of little things that
we've been thinking about.

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:14.530
And I guess finally,
I'll start what

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:18.010
we're going to do between
now and before we go.

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:23.000
In this past one week, we've
basically divvied up the work.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:24.918
So we've become experts
in our little project.

00:32:24.918 --> 00:32:26.710
But one thing that we
definitely want to do

00:32:26.710 --> 00:32:29.500
is read up on each
other's backgrounds, all

00:32:29.500 --> 00:32:33.610
the literature, catch each other
up to speed on every project.

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:35.770
One thing that Amelia,
which is our trip leader,

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:38.590
suggested is that we do
some back-of-the-envelope

00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:41.800
calculations, maybe take that
with us, have that on hand,

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:44.020
because that's one of the
technical skills that are

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:44.620
valuable.

00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:46.670
That's why we're there.

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:48.550
And so just to keep
those numbers in mind.

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:52.277
And anything else,
Liz, for your projects?

00:32:52.277 --> 00:32:54.110
GUEST SPEAKER: We've
had some correspondence

00:32:54.110 --> 00:32:56.000
with our community partners.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.022
And one of the
things we asked her

00:32:58.022 --> 00:32:59.480
that I thought was
really important

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:02.300
was, was there going to be
an opportunity for us to cook

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:03.660
with the families?

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.210
And I think that'll be a
really good experience.

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:07.830
And she said there
will be, of course.

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:09.538
But that will be a
really good experience

00:33:09.538 --> 00:33:12.900
because that will just help us
strengthen our relationships

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:13.890
with the community.

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:16.680
And also I think
the question that we

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:18.810
want to know from
the solar cookers is,

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:20.940
are they using the
current solar cook now?

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:25.890
And what are the benefits of
them using their wood charcoal

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:29.850
methods or however they're
cooking predominantly now,

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:31.137
versus using the solar cooker.

00:33:31.137 --> 00:33:32.720
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah,
it should give us

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:34.820
a really good opportunity
both for the charcoal aspect

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:36.320
and for the solar
cooker aspect just

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:38.630
to see what they need to
cook, how often they cook,

00:33:38.630 --> 00:33:40.640
how long it takes
right now, are they

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:42.740
cooking indoors or
outdoors, just everything.

00:33:42.740 --> 00:33:45.270
And so if we cook with them,
it'll both strengthen the bond

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:47.103
but also answer all
those questions we have.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:53.905
GUEST SPEAKER: And that's us.

00:33:53.905 --> 00:33:56.875
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.230
AMY BANZAERT: So some thoughts--

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.780
you might be wanting
to bring a thermometer

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:06.330
and/or thermocouple
data logger with you

00:34:06.330 --> 00:34:09.499
for doing some of your
stove measurements.

00:34:09.499 --> 00:34:11.166
GUEST SPEAKER: What
was that last thing?

00:34:11.166 --> 00:34:12.150
A thermocouple what?

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:13.608
AMY BANZAERT: With
the data logger.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:18.900
There's a book on
the Jiko stove, which

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:22.560
is the stove with the
ceramic in it, that explains

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:24.540
pretty clearly how
to construct it

00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:27.510
as well as the specifications
for the ceramic,

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:32.969
because only certain types
of clay work for a Jiko

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:34.630
and provide good insulation.

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.260
And I don't know what will
be available in Nicaragua.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:44.639
It's rather different as
a continent than Africa.

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:47.790
So I don't know how
that'll compare.

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:50.909
I know some feedback that
I've heard on the stoves

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:53.159
is just a different
height and/or size,

00:34:53.159 --> 00:34:55.110
that the original
design was designed

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:57.450
to go inside their
existing wood stoves.

00:34:57.450 --> 00:35:00.480
And a lot of women preferred
for it to be freestanding.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:04.260
So that would be another
area of investigation.

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:07.200
For the cell phone charger, I
think definitely investigating

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:12.840
whether individual chargers or
a shared charger is preferable

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:16.110
and figuring that out.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:18.530
It's hard to say at this point
what people would actually

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:19.030
want.

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:21.480
And so really spending the
time asking the questions

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:22.950
to learn that.

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:24.420
And also phone usage--

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:29.190
like you just mentioned,
standard stuff,

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:32.850
is that most of us probably
charge our phones overnight

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:34.360
and then use them
during the day.

00:35:34.360 --> 00:35:36.370
Is that what their
usage looks like?

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:37.890
And is that their
ideal scenario?

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:40.380
Or is charging during
the day and using it

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:43.320
during the night preferable?

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.650
There's no telling without
asking those questions.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:49.680
And I think Amelia's suggestion
of running through those

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:51.180
estimations and
back-of-the-envelope

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:54.422
calculations that you have them
on hand to modify and adapt

00:35:54.422 --> 00:35:56.880
while you're in the field is
a great one actually for every

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:58.740
team.

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:00.660
GUEST SPEAKER: I had
