00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:17.790
that he was researching that.

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:19.090
So I was like, oh,
that's not out yet.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:19.230
GUEST SPEAKER: No, it is.

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:21.160
AMY BANZAERT: No,
it is actually.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:23.080
GUEST SPEAKER: Aren't
there solar panels in--

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:25.330
GUEST SPEAKER: I
should look it up.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.550
AMY BANZAERT: I don't
think this selected

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:29.060
chain uses the flexible ones.

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:33.190
They just have small
ones, because the problem

00:38:33.190 --> 00:38:36.190
with the flexible ones is
their efficiency is lower,

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:39.842
but they are great.

00:38:39.842 --> 00:38:41.800
So and then the only
other thing to think about

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:44.410
if you're going to do wearables
is thinking about where you're

00:38:44.410 --> 00:38:48.610
actually going to get direct
sunlight because are people

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:49.810
outside enough?

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:55.900
And the idea of putting the
iPhone charger on your pocket

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:59.680
concerns me from how much direct
sunlight are you going to get,

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:01.810
because if you
recall, you need to be

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.130
perpendicular to your panel.

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:07.510
And so sunlight tends not to be
perpendicular to a human body.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:08.010
[CHUCKLES]

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:13.360
So yeah, so just
thinking about that,

00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:17.530
that's one of the challenges
with wearable solar panels

00:39:17.530 --> 00:39:20.933
is making sure that they're
actually in the sun enough.

00:39:20.933 --> 00:39:23.350
GUEST SPEAKER: I thought that
one consideration might be--

00:39:23.350 --> 00:39:25.390
we were studying
this with the water,

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:27.857
I think the first lab we
did of carrying water.

00:39:27.857 --> 00:39:29.440
If they're actually
making long trips,

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:31.660
that's a pretty
good time to just--

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.280
if they're carrying
it on their backs,

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:34.310
if they're carrying
the load on the back,

00:39:34.310 --> 00:39:36.393
then put something like a
sombrero on or something

00:39:36.393 --> 00:39:38.230
and put the solar
panel on top, and then

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:39.272
the water and the charge.

00:39:39.272 --> 00:39:41.710
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so I
