I'm Benzi.

00:40:07.808 --> 00:40:08.850
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm Yulia.

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:09.670
GUEST SPEAKER: Bessie.

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:10.090
GUEST SPEAKER: Adrian.

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:11.530
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm Benji.

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:13.750
GUEST SPEAKER: So we haven't
had much communication

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:16.570
with ASAPROSAR yet because
they're a new community

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:18.350
partner in El Salvador.

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:21.520
So some of our projects
are kind of just our ideas

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:24.610
and what we would like to see
or would like to implement.

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:26.190
It's not necessarily
the projects

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:27.940
that we're actually
going to be working on

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:29.002
while we're down there.

00:40:29.002 --> 00:40:30.460
We know that we're
definitely going

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:32.668
to be doing a charcoal burn,
but other than that it's

00:40:32.668 --> 00:40:33.910
kind of up in the air.

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:38.710
But ASAPROSAR has a
focus on rural areas

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:39.950
and needy communities.

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:42.880
So we're thinking that we're
going to be in a rural area--

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:43.570
still not sure.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:48.220
[CHUCKLES] So we aren't totally
sure about the exact projects.

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:50.520
We're looking at
ventilation for cooking

00:40:50.520 --> 00:40:55.180
in the houses, charcoal stoves,
and lighting improvements.

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:58.090
So for instance, with
lighting improvements,

00:40:58.090 --> 00:41:00.080
we don't know what kind
of lights they have.

00:41:00.080 --> 00:41:01.538
So these are
questions that we need

00:41:01.538 --> 00:41:03.460
to ask when we're down there.

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:05.380
GUEST SPEAKER: So
just to introduce some

00:41:05.380 --> 00:41:06.880
of the things we looked at.

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:09.940
We just created a library
with an image, and then

00:41:09.940 --> 00:41:13.060
just key phrases of
what these things are.

00:41:13.060 --> 00:41:15.670
And as Benzi said, I
think most of our time

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:17.650
there would actually
be asking questions.

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:19.192
We've come up with
a lot of questions

00:41:19.192 --> 00:41:22.210
just to really get to
know what people need,

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:23.830
and then work with
them, as opposed

00:41:23.830 --> 00:41:27.820
to come up with some idea
and just try to impose it.

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:30.580
But so in terms
of the ventilation

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:35.530
or using stoves that
make a lot less smoke,

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:37.810
we've looked at, for
example, the first image

00:41:37.810 --> 00:41:42.240
is the solar chimney, which
is a hollow wall that's

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:45.330
built into the space that
pushes the hot air out

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:48.700
and cool air comes from the
other side of the house.

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:51.150
So I guess simple
spatial techniques

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:53.260
that can create more airflow.

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:54.750
Depending on where
the stove is, we

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:58.710
can get a lot of the
smoke out of the house.

00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:02.940
Some of the stoves we looked
at were the Upesi stove

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:04.200
is one of the first ones.

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:06.720
And it's clay.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:08.170
A lot of them were very similar.

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:10.890
And some of them were
mentioned from previous groups,

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:15.600
but a clay stove that usually
has an opening on the bottom

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:17.790
and a metal piece on top.

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:20.940
The clay is used for a
little bit more insulation

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:23.910
than just a sheet metal.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:28.560
And this, it's made
with ceramic or mud.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:31.810
And it's something
is made by women.

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:34.800
And it could be sold for profit.

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:39.240
A couple of the solar
cookers are another idea.

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:41.850
And they were mentioned before.

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:45.510
The two that I looked
at, one was actually

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:49.170
an MIT project done a couple
of years ago, I think.

00:42:49.170 --> 00:42:53.520
And so both of them
use reflective surfaces

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:57.120
to get the light and
focus it onto a surface

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:01.260
to heat up the food.

00:43:01.260 --> 00:43:05.220
The Ecocina and then the
other one, the ONIL stove,

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.300
they're pretty similar.

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:11.340
They're cast from
cement or concrete.

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:15.930
They are similar
to one of the Upesi

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:18.210
above was that they
have an opening

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:20.910
on the bottom and
the metal plate

00:43:20.910 --> 00:43:24.450
on top that's used
to heat the food.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:28.440
They produce a lot less smoke
and, because of the insulation,

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:34.300
the fuel is actually reduced
by about 60%, which is great.

00:43:34.300 --> 00:43:37.560
The one on the bottom is an
improved three-stone fire

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:38.640
stove.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:41.250
And all it is building--

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:44.700
it was a series of
steps used to show

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:50.490
how you can actually build
a stove with clay and using

00:43:50.490 --> 00:43:50.990
the bricks.

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:52.430
And it's not portable.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:56.990
It's going to be built and
used where you want it.

00:43:56.990 --> 00:44:01.190
But it has an opening
on top, and then

00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:06.870
usually a hole on the bottom for
airflow and to feed the fuel.

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:09.510
Some of the charcoal
stoves that we looked at--

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:11.220
and we've talked about these.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:17.350
The Jiko, this other one below,
is a very similar design, just

00:44:17.350 --> 00:44:20.960
a little different.

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:24.230
This one uses clay,
and it has the holes.

00:44:24.230 --> 00:44:26.160
And the one on the
bottom is, again,

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:29.990
something that we saw
that you can control

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:32.210
the airflow by the
different openings, the door

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:34.430
openings, or openings
on the bottom

00:44:34.430 --> 00:44:38.630
or on the sides,
depending on, I guess,

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:41.090
how much we need it to
heat up and how much air we

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:43.512
want to let in.

00:44:43.512 --> 00:44:46.000
In terms of the lighting,
this first project

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:48.620
was actually the portable
light that I mentioned.

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:52.060
And what they do
is they make a bag,

00:44:52.060 --> 00:44:54.807
and they put these
flexible solar cells on it

00:44:54.807 --> 00:44:56.890
so that the women, when
they carry it, it charges.

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:59.570
And at night when
you open it up,

00:44:59.570 --> 00:45:04.020
it uses these reflective and
diffused surfaces actually

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:08.460
to make the most use out of
the light source that they use.

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:10.080
And it's usually
used for studying.

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:12.930
It has enough light
to study at night.

00:45:12.930 --> 00:45:15.780
Some of the other light
sources we looked at

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:21.300
were solar lights mostly,
whether portable or stationary,

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:23.390
something that can be
charged during the day

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:25.110
and used at night.

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:28.260
And they come in so many
different varieties--

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:31.780
flashlights, these bottom ones
you can hang from the ceiling,

00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:33.380
you can carry anywhere with you.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:39.480
They're all, in a way, common,
that they use a solar panel

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:43.010
to charge, and then the actual
light source is different.

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:48.505
But actually the idea that
the information that we

00:45:48.505 --> 00:45:50.630
know about the communities
is that they actually do

00:45:50.630 --> 00:45:55.820
have access to the grid, but
they have very few light bulbs,

00:45:55.820 --> 00:45:59.350
or I guess I don't have
that much power out of it.

00:45:59.350 --> 00:46:01.520
We're not really sure yet.

00:46:01.520 --> 00:46:03.400
So in terms of
looking at precedents,

00:46:03.400 --> 00:46:06.550
we weren't really sure
what these things could be.

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:10.450
But we have some ideas of how
we can use as little light

00:46:10.450 --> 00:46:12.620
as they have in
order to brighten up

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:13.870
this space as much as we want.

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:19.220
And these are some of our ideas.

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:21.515
GUEST SPEAKER: OK, so
as Yulia said earlier,

00:46:21.515 --> 00:46:23.390
one of the main problems
in these communities

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:26.030
is the existence of
poor ventilation.

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:28.820
And a lot of these
houses use wood burning,

00:46:28.820 --> 00:46:31.550
which as you know, when
wood burns it releases

00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:33.140
a lot of toxins in the air.

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:37.550
So our ideas were mainly to
serve to move the smoke out

00:46:37.550 --> 00:46:40.970
of the house or to clean the
smoke as it leaves the house.

00:46:40.970 --> 00:46:44.960
So one of the ideas that we
had was the smoke dispenser,

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:49.430
which the way it works is
the pot is put on the stove

00:46:49.430 --> 00:46:55.970
so that as the pot covers
any way for the smoke

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:59.870
to be released into the
house and instead it

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:04.100
forces the smoke to go
outward to some place

00:47:04.100 --> 00:47:05.570
outside of the house.

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:08.450
And we also employ the
use of these rings, which

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:13.490
serve to accommodate
different sizes of pots

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:14.660
that they may have.

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:19.190
And another idea that we
had was the smoke filter,

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:23.900
where the idea was as the
smoke rises over the stove,

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:26.730
it would pass through
possibly a wet towel,

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:30.545
and the wet towel would clean
the smoke as it would leave.

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:37.310
So a couple of the
specs that we noted

00:47:37.310 --> 00:47:40.040
were, of course, low cost.

00:47:40.040 --> 00:47:41.860
Of course, these
places have low income,

00:47:41.860 --> 00:47:44.450
so they have to be
affordable to these people.

00:47:44.450 --> 00:47:45.890
It has to be easy to assemble.

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:47.432
You don't want
something that's going

00:47:47.432 --> 00:47:48.990
to take six months to assemble.

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:52.910
So we decided that it should
take less than two days

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:55.160
to put together.

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:57.500
Ease of repair was
very important.

00:47:57.500 --> 00:47:59.803
In case any of these
things break down,

00:47:59.803 --> 00:48:01.220
it should be easy
for these people

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:06.260
to get parts to
repair their stove.

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:08.270
It should be effective.

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:11.540
It should be able to increase
the air quality of the house

00:48:11.540 --> 00:48:14.810
by about 60%.

00:48:14.810 --> 00:48:17.870
It shouldn't interfere
with their cooking.

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:21.972
A lot of people,
they like the space

00:48:21.972 --> 00:48:23.930
that they have around
their pots because that's

00:48:23.930 --> 00:48:31.550
what they're used to, which
is why also we made the smoke

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:34.640
filter so that it was
able to be stowed away,

00:48:34.640 --> 00:48:41.420
just for possible aesthetically
pleasing to the users.

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:42.590
Should be easy to use.

00:48:42.590 --> 00:48:45.590
Obviously these are things that
these people are used to using,

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:49.290
and so it should be easy for
them to get used to using them.

00:48:49.290 --> 00:48:51.410
And it should be
easy to maintain.

00:48:51.410 --> 00:48:54.530
For example, with the
filter, shouldn't have

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:58.250
to be replaced after every use.

00:48:58.250 --> 00:49:03.140
We estimated that maybe about
less than three times a week.

00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:05.700
A couple of
questions that we had

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:08.615
that will make us more able
to solve these problems

00:49:08.615 --> 00:49:11.900
is, what's the family's
average income?

00:49:11.900 --> 00:49:14.090
And how much are they
willing to pay out

00:49:14.090 --> 00:49:17.030
of their average income
for these stoves?

00:49:17.030 --> 00:49:19.460
Where in the house
is the stove located?

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:22.040
And is it movable?

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:26.570
And can we possibly put
it outside of the house?

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:31.430
What is already being used
in their homes to ventilate?

00:49:31.430 --> 00:49:38.600
What materials are available,
such as the metal and curtains

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:42.620
that could be used
for our ideas?

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:47.210
We also wanted to know how
they store their pots and pans,

00:49:47.210 --> 00:49:50.420
and if we can incorporate
that into our ideas.

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:55.490
And we also wanted to know
what the house setup was like.

00:49:55.490 --> 00:49:58.380
Are their windows the
openings for the smoke outlet,

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:01.700
instead of us having to
break a hole in their wall?

00:50:01.700 --> 00:50:04.910
We figured they
might not like that.

00:50:04.910 --> 00:50:07.490
Benji's now going to talk
a little about the charcoal

00:50:07.490 --> 00:50:09.270
stoves.

00:50:09.270 --> 00:50:11.460
GUEST SPEAKER: All right,
so a lot of the ideas

00:50:11.460 --> 00:50:12.270
are very similar.

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:15.720
As we saw, they have
different features

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.520
that we thought would
be useful, depending

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:19.350
on what the preferences
of the users were.

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:22.560
And so we just attacked it from
a general standpoint of what

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:24.330
the general shape of
the stove would be

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:27.420
and what the key feature would
be that we wanted to highlight?

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:29.280
And so these are two of them.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:32.458
The first one is basically like
a sealed steamer that sort of

00:50:32.458 --> 00:50:33.750
looks like a vegetable steamer.

00:50:33.750 --> 00:50:35.333
I don't if you're
familiar with those,

00:50:35.333 --> 00:50:36.840
that they open and close.

00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:38.100
They have overlapping layers.

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:42.060
And so it can basically
accommodate just a variety

00:50:42.060 --> 00:50:43.860
of sizes of pots and
pans, but it still

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:47.280
maintains the seal all
the way around the pan,

00:50:47.280 --> 00:50:49.050
assuming that it's circular.

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:50.170
And so that's the idea.

00:50:50.170 --> 00:50:53.340
And we don't have any
specific designs for that,

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:56.010
but you could
incorporate the airflow

00:50:56.010 --> 00:51:00.280
or the insulation as needed.

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:02.880
So we didn't really focus
on the specifics of it.

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:04.380
The second one that
we thought about

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:05.838
is actually very
similar to the one

00:51:05.838 --> 00:51:07.810
that we saw back there,
before we saw it.

00:51:07.810 --> 00:51:11.857
It's a funnel, basically, that
has a small enough opening

00:51:11.857 --> 00:51:13.440
at the bottom and a
steep enough angle

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:15.617
so that the charcoal gets
jammed within the funnel.

00:51:15.617 --> 00:51:17.325
It's something that
happens all the time,

00:51:17.325 --> 00:51:19.700
like if you have marbles or
anything, if they get jammed.

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:21.825
And so this allows
for airflow to come up

00:51:21.825 --> 00:51:23.700
and for ashes to fall
down but for the larger

00:51:23.700 --> 00:51:25.890
pieces of charcoal to stay.

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:29.250
And so we thought that was just
an effective but simple way

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:34.283
of keeping the charcoal in and
having just a simpler design.

00:51:34.283 --> 00:51:35.700
And it also
accommodates a variety

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:40.000
of pots and pans because of
the funnel as it goes out.

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:43.200
So the things that we wanted
to make sure and maintain

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:46.650
were its effectiveness
as a stove.

00:51:46.650 --> 00:51:49.200
So we wanted it to cook
the food just as well.

00:51:49.200 --> 00:51:51.480
Who would want to switch to
a stove that doesn't cook

00:51:51.480 --> 00:51:53.310
as well as your other one?

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:54.900
So that one is
definitely number one.

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:58.020
And we wanted to maintain
its fuel efficiency--

00:51:58.020 --> 00:51:59.400
not using any more
fuel than they

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:01.770
do right now, because
there's no reason

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:04.830
to switch to stove that's
less fuel efficient.

00:52:04.830 --> 00:52:09.600
And we also want it to be
affordable so that there's

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:10.860
a reward for switching.

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:14.670
And also pleasing aesthetically,
and that it accommodates

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:17.700
many pots and pans so that there
aren't any barriers to keeping

00:52:17.700 --> 00:52:20.160
people from using them.

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:22.760
So the questions
mostly revolved around

00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:25.640
what currently
exists in the places.

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:28.400
Like we said, we don't really
know much about our partners

00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:30.872
there, so we'll
mostly be asking me

00:52:30.872 --> 00:52:32.330
about materials
that are available,

00:52:32.330 --> 00:52:35.900
like what they usually
use, what their habits are

00:52:35.900 --> 00:52:39.110
in terms of cooking, and
why they don't use charcoal,

00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:40.380
or how much they pay for fuel.

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:42.470
So all these basic questions
that really outline

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:45.792
the needs of the community.

00:52:45.792 --> 00:52:47.500
GUEST SPEAKER: So I'm
going to be talking

00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:48.940
about improved lighting.

00:52:48.940 --> 00:52:52.150
And so we basically came
up with two general ideas

00:52:52.150 --> 00:52:53.853
that could be feasible.

00:52:53.853 --> 00:52:55.270
Once again like
we said, we really

00:52:55.270 --> 00:52:58.060
don't a whole lot about what
we're going to be doing at all,

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.340
but this was something
that they recommended

00:53:00.340 --> 00:53:01.780
that we might look into.

00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:03.497
We don't if this
is feasible or not.

00:53:03.497 --> 00:53:05.080
But basically what
we came up with was

00:53:05.080 --> 00:53:08.470
the idea of first a white
room, which is just going

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:10.900
to essentially let sounds--

00:53:10.900 --> 00:53:15.670
we're just going to maximize
them amount of lighting,

00:53:15.670 --> 00:53:16.970
like reflective surfaces.

00:53:16.970 --> 00:53:19.960
So we're thinking about
reflectors on the ceiling,

00:53:19.960 --> 00:53:25.750
having white curtains, blocking
maybe windows and stuff,

00:53:25.750 --> 00:53:28.820
and then painting the
walls white in color

00:53:28.820 --> 00:53:31.520
so that what light
comes into the room,

00:53:31.520 --> 00:53:33.700
it makes the room look
lighter just in general,

00:53:33.700 --> 00:53:36.700
because if you have
dark colors on the walls

00:53:36.700 --> 00:53:38.860
it makes your room look darker.

00:53:38.860 --> 00:53:42.640
The second idea we had was this
concept called the Sunflower.

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:46.180
It's sort of like
an umbrella that

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:51.610
can be used to direct light in
certain areas that you need.

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:54.670
It can swivel and
go up and down based

00:53:54.670 --> 00:53:56.650
on where you want to
direct your light,

00:53:56.650 --> 00:53:58.810
whether you want it to
illuminate most of the room.

00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.360
Or if someone's staying up
later than another person,

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:04.750
perhaps to study or to
prep for activities that

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:06.580
would take place during
the following day,

00:54:06.580 --> 00:54:09.190
you don't want to interfere
with anybody sleeping or such.

00:54:09.190 --> 00:54:11.560
So that is why we
have it directional.

00:54:11.560 --> 00:54:17.350
And you can adjust
how much light's

00:54:17.350 --> 00:54:24.310
directed by twirling it
around, and then also

00:54:24.310 --> 00:54:27.230
by how much you
open and expand it.

00:54:27.230 --> 00:54:30.970
So some project specs we came
up with-- we want, obviously,

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:38.980
it to be very flexible and
to have multiple uses, so

00:54:38.980 --> 00:54:41.210
basically for different
tasks that you might have,

00:54:41.210 --> 00:54:43.870
whether it's for
cooking or for reading.

00:54:43.870 --> 00:54:46.510
The ability to
direct your light, so

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:48.340
where you can turn
the light that you

00:54:48.340 --> 00:54:49.870
choose to be directing.

00:54:49.870 --> 00:54:53.170
We want it to be
affordable and reliable.

00:54:53.170 --> 00:54:57.430
And these are questions
we don't necessarily

00:54:57.430 --> 00:54:59.020
have a value for
because we don't

00:54:59.020 --> 00:55:00.380
know who we're working with.

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:02.440
So we don't know what
would necessarily

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:05.890
be reliable or more feasible.

00:55:05.890 --> 00:55:08.950
And obviously, the efficiency
of light, the quality of light,

00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:12.340
we want it to be able to improve
the lighting by about 50%,

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:15.820
and has as much
brightness as we can.

00:55:15.820 --> 00:55:18.070
So obviously,
we're going to have

00:55:18.070 --> 00:55:21.340
to ask a lot of questions such
as, what types of light bulbs

00:55:21.340 --> 00:55:22.870
do they currently
have available?

00:55:22.870 --> 00:55:26.560
How many light bulbs they
currently have available?

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:28.840
How much power do
they have access to?

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:31.210
How much light does
each customer need?

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:33.850
What type of
activities do they do,

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:36.560
would also contribute to the
type of lighting they really

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:37.060
need.

00:55:37.060 --> 00:55:38.440
Do they just need a
little bit of lighting

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:40.090
for walking around
and such, then it

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:41.060
doesn't have to be as bright.

00:55:41.060 --> 00:55:43.352
Both if they're reading and
they're studying, obviously

00:55:43.352 --> 00:55:45.310
you're going to need a
lot more direct light.

00:55:45.310 --> 00:55:49.240
And also how much the
customer is willing to pay.

00:55:49.240 --> 00:55:53.680
And also one of our main ideas
was the idea of a white room,

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:55.750
but are they going
to even have access

00:55:55.750 --> 00:55:58.060
to white and lighter
colored materials.

00:55:58.060 --> 00:56:00.670
Obviously that's going to make
the entire design moot if they

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:02.650
don't.

00:56:02.650 --> 00:56:06.200
And where is their light
bulb currently located?

00:56:06.200 --> 00:56:09.190
Is it wired into their house?

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:11.350
Or is it more of
a portable nature?

00:56:11.350 --> 00:56:15.460
And then do we need direct
daylight into dark spaces?

00:56:15.460 --> 00:56:19.160
So those are some of the
questions we were going to ask.

00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:21.607
And so we have some
expected activities.

00:56:21.607 --> 00:56:23.690
GUEST SPEAKER: So while
we're down in El Salvador,

00:56:23.690 --> 00:56:26.750
we're going to do a
charcoal burn demo.

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:29.530
We're hoping to
first demo to a group

00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:31.280
of people that are
more interested in this

00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:34.340
who could learn and be like
our liaisons to the community.

00:56:34.340 --> 00:56:36.560
So they would learn
firsthand, and then

00:56:36.560 --> 00:56:40.490
help us with the second demo
to the larger community.

00:56:40.490 --> 00:56:42.860
And then we want to talk
about cooking indoors

00:56:42.860 --> 00:56:44.600
and find out what
they're actually doing

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:47.480
and if they're aware of these
dangers, talk about charcoal

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:51.230
usage and how our charcoal
burn would be effective if they

00:56:51.230 --> 00:56:53.180
switch to charcoal stoves.

00:56:53.180 --> 00:56:56.010
And then just discuss what
they use for lighting now

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:59.030
and figure out how we can
improve upon what they have.

00:56:59.030 --> 00:57:02.510
So a lot of it's just going to
be investigating in the field

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:04.490
and learning about
their living habits

00:57:04.490 --> 00:57:07.020
and how they work with
what they have now.

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:14.924
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:15.912 --> 00:57:18.876
GUEST SPEAKER: Any questions?

00:57:18.876 --> 00:57:23.322
GUEST SPEAKER: For the one with
the ventilation with the hand

00:57:23.322 --> 00:57:27.520
towel, wet towel,
how often do you

00:57:27.520 --> 00:57:30.442
plan on replacing the filter.

00:57:30.442 --> 00:57:33.587
Or are you always
using the same towel?

00:57:33.587 --> 00:57:35.170
GUEST SPEAKER: So
the idea was that it

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:39.800
would have to be replaced maybe
less than three times a week.

00:57:39.800 --> 00:57:43.660
So maybe every
other day at most.

00:57:43.660 --> 00:57:46.390
And as far as
cleanable, I mean, I

00:57:46.390 --> 00:57:49.420
guess it could be cleanable
it it's a wet towel.

00:57:49.420 --> 00:57:52.330
But we still need
to look into that.

00:57:52.330 --> 00:57:54.580
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, I mean,
we're not filter experts,

00:57:54.580 --> 00:57:57.803
and the particulates are
really, really small.

00:57:57.803 --> 00:57:59.470
That's what makes
them really dangerous.

00:57:59.470 --> 00:58:03.160
And so a wet towel
wouldn't necessarily

00:58:03.160 --> 00:58:06.340
take the very dangerous
things, but we don't know.

00:58:06.340 --> 00:58:10.020
What if this simple
thing could really help,

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:13.070
so we haven't really done any
specific research into how

00:58:13.070 --> 00:58:16.308
to improve filter
efficiency, but yeah, that's

00:58:16.308 --> 00:58:20.250
the basic idea.

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:24.080
AMY BANZAERT: So before
proposing that idea,

00:58:24.080 --> 00:58:27.890
I wouldn't propose an idea
that you don't actually

00:58:27.890 --> 00:58:31.748
know if a wet towel will provide
any effective filtration.

00:58:31.748 --> 00:58:34.040
So it's something you could
look into is this feasible,

00:58:34.040 --> 00:58:36.680
but I wouldn't discuss
it as an idea that

00:58:36.680 --> 00:58:38.810
has merit to the people
that you're working

00:58:38.810 --> 00:58:41.580
with until you know if it does.

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:44.000
And from an
experimental standpoint,

00:58:44.000 --> 00:58:47.990
we have a capability to run
emissions tests in the lab.

00:58:47.990 --> 00:58:50.720
I don't if we can pull off
running it before Sunday,

00:58:50.720 --> 00:58:55.580
but we might be able to, or
certainly when you get back.

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:59.960
You should definitely-- it seems
like are well prepared, being

00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:02.362
prepared just to be
flexible and figuring out

00:59:02.362 --> 00:59:03.320
what's going to happen.

00:59:03.320 --> 00:59:05.780
I think you did a great job
given the lack of information

00:59:05.780 --> 00:59:07.670
you had.

00:59:07.670 --> 00:59:11.870
Along those lines, I would try
calling the community partners

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:12.590
today.

00:59:12.590 --> 00:59:16.280
There's two that we have phone
numbers for, so call them both.

00:59:16.280 --> 00:59:18.080
Does anyone speak
Spanish on the team?

00:59:20.442 --> 00:59:22.400
GUEST SPEAKER: Well, we
two do, but we're not--

00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:22.550
AMY BANZAERT: Right.

00:59:22.550 --> 00:59:24.680
But you could but you could
help on the phone today.

00:59:24.680 --> 00:59:25.680
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, yeah.

00:59:25.680 --> 00:59:29.000
AMY BANZAERT: So that
would be good to start.

00:59:29.000 --> 00:59:33.290
And then finally,
just stove quality--

00:59:33.290 --> 00:59:35.390
there was a lot of
designs there and some

00:59:35.390 --> 00:59:37.350
I think have more
merit than others.

00:59:37.350 --> 00:59:39.560
And this is true for
Grupo Fenix as well

00:59:39.560 --> 00:59:43.280
to think about, that you should
definitely check out those two

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:46.910
links I put on the
charcoal lecture, which

00:59:46.910 --> 00:59:50.270
is an organization
called Aprovecho,

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:52.820
and then I think it's
called cookstoves.net.

00:59:52.820 --> 00:59:54.800
Because there's a
ton of cookstove

00:59:54.800 --> 00:59:57.590
designs out there, some
of which are really great,

00:59:57.590 --> 01:00:00.110
like the JICO, and others
of which are getting

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:04.950
sold and do not actually improve
efficiency or emissions at all.

01:00:04.950 --> 01:00:07.280
And it's really easy to
just say my stove is better

01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:11.120
and get our website up and get
some funders without actually

01:00:11.120 --> 01:00:14.220
doing any due diligence that
your stove is, in fact, better.

01:00:14.220 --> 01:00:18.110
And so it's easy to make
a stove that's worse.

01:00:18.110 --> 01:00:22.460
And you don't want
to be doing that.

01:00:22.460 --> 01:00:23.297
So cool.

01:00:23.297 --> 01:00:24.130
That's all I've got.

01:00:24.130 --> 01:00:24.820
Thanks, guys.

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:30.870
[APPLAUSE]

